      ['name' => 'U Street', 'blurb' => 'Historic corridor known for live music, nightlife, and restaurants, with a Green and Yellow Line station. Lively and well connected.'],
      ['name' => 'Arlington, VA', 'blurb' => 'Across the Potomac, neighborhoods like Clarendon, Ballston, and Rosslyn line the Metro\'s Orange and Silver lines, with high-rise apartments and quick access to downtown DC.'],
    ],
    'prices' => '<p>DC is one of the pricier markets in the country, especially near Metro stations in central neighborhoods. Furnished private rooms in group houses and shared apartments commonly advertise somewhere in the $1,100&ndash;$1,900 per month range, while entire furnished studios and one-bedrooms typically run higher, often $2,500 or more in popular areas.</p><p>These ranges are approximate, not official data. Prices vary by season, neighborhood and furnishing, and summer intern season is the most competitive, so compare current listings early.</p>',
    'transit'=> '<p>WMATA&rsquo;s Metrorail system has six color-coded lines &mdash; Red, Orange, Silver, Blue, Yellow, and Green &mdash; reaching across DC and into Maryland and Virginia, backed by an extensive Metrobus network. Capital Bikeshare stations are everywhere, and many residents get by without a car. Union Station connects to Amtrak, MARC, and Virginia Railway Express.</p><p>The region has three airports: Reagan National (on the Metro\'s Blue and Yellow lines), Dulles International (on the Silver Line), and BWI Thurgood Marshall near Baltimore, reachable by MARC or Amtrak.</p>',
    'tips' => [
      'interns'  => 'Hill and agency internships typically start in late May or June, and demand for summer rooms near Capitol Hill, Foggy Bottom, and Dupont is intense. Start searching early in the spring, and consider Metro-accessible neighborhoods a few stops out for better value.',
      'nurses'   => 'Major employers include MedStar Washington Hospital Center, MedStar Georgetown University Hospital, George Washington University Hospital, Children\'s National Hospital, and Howard University Hospital. Walter Reed National Military Medical Center is just outside the city in Bethesda.',
      'students' => 'Georgetown, George Washington University, American University, Howard University, and Catholic University all generate sublet supply when students leave for the summer. GW and Georgetown summer sublets are especially popular with interns.',
      'seasonal' => 'Summer is by far the peak season, driven by internships. Demand also follows the congressional calendar and academic year, and the weeks around a new administration can bring extra movement.',
    ],
    'faqs' => [
      ['q' => 'When should I start looking for a summer intern sublet in DC?', 'a' => 'Many interns begin searching in March or April for a late May or June start. Rooms near Capitol Hill and Metro stations go first, so earlier is better.'],
      ['q' => 'Is it worth living in Arlington instead of DC?', 'a' => 'For many short-term renters, yes. Neighborhoods along the Orange and Silver lines in Arlington are a quick Metro ride to downtown DC and can offer newer buildings. Just check your commute to your specific office.'],
      ['q' => 'Do I need a car in Washington, DC?', 'a' => 'Usually not. Metro, buses, bike share, and walkable neighborhoods make car-free living easy, and parking can be scarce and expensive.'],
      ['q' => 'What is a group house in DC?', 'a' => 'A group house is a rowhouse or single-family home shared by several unrelated renters, each with a private room. They are common in DC and are often a more affordable way to stay in a central neighborhood.'],
    ],
  ],

  'pa/philadelphia' => [
    'name'   => 'Philadelphia',
    'state'  => 'PA',
    'type'   => 'metro',
    'lat'    => 39.9526, 'lng' => -75.1652,
    'tagline'=> 'Furnished rowhouse rooms and apartments for students, clinicians, and interns, from University City to Fishtown and South Philly.',
    'intro'  => '<p>Philadelphia offers a walkable, historic city core at prices that are often well below New York or Boston, which makes it a favorite for short-term renters. Its housing is dominated by brick rowhouses, many divided into apartments or shared by roommates, along with converted industrial lofts and newer buildings in Center City and along the rivers.</p><p>Short-term demand is driven heavily by the city&rsquo;s universities and hospitals. Penn, Drexel, Temple, and Jefferson bring students, researchers, and medical trainees who need housing by the semester or rotation, and the large health systems employ many travel nurses. Summer sublets are plentiful near campuses when students leave town. Short-term renters also like that Philadelphia sits between New York and Washington on the Northeast Corridor, so weekend trips to either city are easy by train.</p><p>Center City is compact enough to walk across, and SEPTA&rsquo;s subway, trolley, and regional rail lines connect most neighborhoods. <strong>Ask about heating and cooling</strong>: older rowhouses vary a lot, and window air conditioners are common.</p>',
    'neighborhoods' => [
      ['name' => 'Rittenhouse Square', 'blurb' => 'Upscale Center City neighborhood around one of the city\'s best-loved parks, with brownstones, high-rises, and restaurants. Walkable to most downtown offices.'],
      ['name' => 'University City', 'blurb' => 'West Philadelphia neighborhood home to Penn, Drexel, and major hospitals, served by the Market-Frankford Line and trolleys. The center of student and medical sublets.'],
      ['name' => 'Fishtown', 'blurb' => 'Former industrial neighborhood turned dining and music hub, with rowhouses and new construction. The Market-Frankford Line reaches Center City quickly.'],
      ['name' => 'Northern Liberties', 'blurb' => 'Next to Fishtown, with converted warehouses, newer apartments, and easy access to Center City.'],
      ['name' => 'Graduate Hospital', 'blurb' => 'Residential rowhouse neighborhood just south of Rittenhouse, popular with young professionals and medical staff.'],
      ['name' => 'East Passyunk', 'blurb' => 'South Philly neighborhood centered on a diagonal avenue of restaurants and shops, with rowhouse living and a strong local feel.'],
      ['name' => 'Manayunk', 'blurb' => 'Hilly riverside neighborhood in Northwest Philadelphia with a lively Main Street and regional rail access.'],
    ],
    'prices' => '<p>Philadelphia is generally more affordable than other large Northeast cities. Furnished private rooms in shared rowhouses and apartments commonly advertise somewhere in the $850&ndash;$1,400 per month range, while entire furnished one-bedrooms in Center City and University City typically run higher, often $1,800 or more.</p><p>These are rough ranges, not official data. Prices vary by season, neighborhood and furnishing, and demand near campuses shifts with the academic calendar, so compare current listings before deciding.</p>',
    'transit'=> '<p>SEPTA runs the city&rsquo;s transit, including the Market-Frankford Line (east-west), the Broad Street Line (north-south), trolley lines into West and Southwest Philadelphia, an extensive bus network, and Regional Rail to the suburbs and the airport. PATCO trains connect Center City to southern New Jersey.</p><p>Philadelphia International Airport is a short Regional Rail ride from Center City. Amtrak&rsquo;s 30th Street Station offers quick trips to New York and Washington, which makes Philly a practical base for frequent travelers.</p>',
    'tips' => [
      'interns'  => 'Many internships are based in Center City and University City, both walkable and well served by SEPTA. Penn and Drexel summer sublets are a good source of affordable furnished rooms.',
      'nurses'   => 'Major employers include Penn Medicine (including the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania), Jefferson Health, Children\'s Hospital of Philadelphia, and Temple University Hospital. University City and Center City are convenient for most of them.',
      'students' => 'The University of Pennsylvania, Drexel, Temple, Thomas Jefferson University, and Saint Joseph\'s University are all in the city, with Villanova and others in the suburbs. Summer sublets near Penn and Drexel are especially common.',
      'seasonal' => 'Demand follows the academic calendar, with sublet supply rising in May and competition picking up in late summer before fall semesters. Summer is also busy with interns.',
    ],
    'faqs' => [
      ['q' => 'Which Philadelphia neighborhoods are best for a short stay without a car?', 'a' => 'Center City, Rittenhouse, University City, Fishtown, and Northern Liberties are all walkable and well served by SEPTA. Many residents live car-free in these areas.'],
      ['q' => 'Is Philadelphia a good base for commuting to New York?', 'a' => 'Some people do it, using Amtrak or NJ Transit connections, though it is a long daily commute. It works better for occasional trips than for a daily job.'],
      ['q' => 'Are rowhouse rooms common in Philadelphia sublets?', 'a' => 'Yes. Many sublets are rooms in shared rowhouses, especially near universities. Ask about heating, cooling, and laundry, since these vary between older homes.'],
    ],
  ],

  'fl/miami' => [
    'name'   => 'Miami',
    'state'  => 'FL',
    'type'   => 'metro',
    'lat'    => 25.7617, 'lng' => -80.1918,
    'tagline'=> 'Furnished condos and rooms for seasonal stays, remote workers, and medical assignments across Miami\'s neighborhoods and nearby beaches.',
    'intro'  => '<p>Miami&rsquo;s rental market moves with the seasons. Winter, roughly December through April, brings snowbirds, visitors escaping colder cities, and a surge in demand for furnished condos. Summer is hotter, more humid, and overlaps with hurricane season, which often softens demand. If your dates are flexible, timing can make a big difference in what you pay and what you can find.</p><p>Much of the city&rsquo;s housing is in high-rise condo buildings, especially in Brickell, Edgewater, and Downtown. Many of these buildings are governed by condo associations that set rules on lease lengths, approvals, and how often units can be rented. <strong>Before you commit, ask whether the building requires an application or has a minimum lease term</strong>, since this can derail a sublet at the last minute.</p><p>Miami draws remote workers, finance and tech transplants, medical staff at the large hospital systems, and students at the University of Miami and FIU. Spanish is widely spoken, and neighborhoods vary enormously in character.</p>',
    'neighborhoods' => [
      ['name' => 'Brickell', 'blurb' => 'Miami\'s financial district, dense with high-rise condos, restaurants, and the free Metromover. Popular with professionals and remote workers.'],
      ['name' => 'Wynwood', 'blurb' => 'Arts district famous for its murals, galleries, breweries, and nightlife, with a growing number of newer apartment buildings.'],
      ['name' => 'Edgewater', 'blurb' => 'Waterfront neighborhood on Biscayne Bay north of Downtown, with high-rise living and views, close to Wynwood and the Design District.'],
      ['name' => 'Coconut Grove', 'blurb' => 'Leafy, laid-back bayfront neighborhood with parks, marinas, and a village center. Metrorail stations are nearby.'],
      ['name' => 'Coral Gables', 'blurb' => 'A separate city with tree-lined streets, Mediterranean-style architecture, and the University of Miami campus. Quieter and more residential.'],
      ['name' => 'Little Havana', 'blurb' => 'Historic heart of Miami\'s Cuban community around Calle Ocho, with older apartment buildings and more modest prices.'],
      ['name' => 'Miami Beach', 'blurb' => 'A separate city across Biscayne Bay, from South Beach to quieter areas farther north. Beach life comes with strict local rules on short-term rentals.'],
    ],
    'prices' => '<p>Miami prices depend heavily on season, building, and proximity to the water. Furnished private rooms commonly advertise somewhere in the $1,100&ndash;$1,900 per month range, while entire furnished condos and one-bedrooms in Brickell, Edgewater, and beachside areas typically run higher, often $2,500 or more, and can rise further in peak winter months.</p><p>These ranges are rough, not official data. Prices vary by season, neighborhood and furnishing, and some buildings charge application fees, so compare current listings and ask about any building costs up front.</p>',
    'transit'=> '<p>Miami-Dade Transit operates Metrorail, which runs from the airport area through Downtown and south toward Dadeland; the free Metromover loops through Downtown, Brickell, and Omni; and an extensive Metrobus network. Brightline trains connect Downtown Miami to Fort Lauderdale, West Palm Beach, and Orlando, while Tri-Rail serves the wider region.</p><p>Miami International Airport is connected to Metrorail, and Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood International is a common alternative. Outside the core, most residents drive, and traffic on the causeways and highways can be heavy.</p>',
    'tips' => [
      'interns'  => 'Many finance, law, and tech internships are in Brickell and Downtown, where the free Metromover makes getting around easy. Summer is low season, so you may find better deals, though it is hot and storm-prone.',
      'nurses'   => 'Major employers include Jackson Health System (anchored by Jackson Memorial Hospital), UHealth at the University of Miami, Baptist Health South Florida, Mount Sinai Medical Center in Miami Beach, and Nicklaus Children\'s Hospital. The Health District near Jackson Memorial is reachable by Metrorail.',
      'students' => 'The University of Miami in Coral Gables, Florida International University, and Miami Dade College are the main institutions. Sublets near UM open up in summer, and Coral Gables and Coconut Grove are popular nearby areas.',
      'seasonal' => 'Winter is high season, with peak demand roughly from December through April, including the crowds around Art Basel in early December. Summer is quieter, partly because it overlaps with hurricane season.',
    ],
    'faqs' => [
      ['q' => 'When is the cheapest time to rent a furnished place in Miami?', 'a' => 'Summer and early fall tend to be softer than winter, when seasonal visitors push up demand. If your dates are flexible, a summer stay may stretch your budget further.'],
      ['q' => 'Do Miami condo buildings allow sublets?', 'a' => 'It depends on the building. Many condo associations require applications or approvals and some set minimum lease terms. Confirm the rules before you pay anything.'],
      ['q' => 'Is Miami Beach part of the City of Miami?', 'a' => 'No. Miami Beach is a separate city with its own rules, including strict limits on short-term rentals in many areas. Coral Gables is also its own city.'],
      ['q' => 'Do I need a car in Miami?', 'a' => 'In Brickell and Downtown, you can manage with Metromover, Metrorail, and ride-shares. Elsewhere, most residents rely on a car.'],
    ],
  ],

  'ga/atlanta' => [
    'name'   => 'Atlanta',
    'state'  => 'GA',
    'type'   => 'metro',
