    'intro'  => '<p>Atlanta is a city of neighborhoods tucked under a heavy tree canopy, and its short-term housing market reflects a diverse mix of renters. Film and television productions bring crews for months at a time, the city&rsquo;s universities and hospitals draw students and clinicians, and Atlanta&rsquo;s role as a corporate hub keeps relocations steady year-round.</p><p>Housing ranges from new mid-rise apartments in Midtown and along the BeltLine to craftsman bungalows in Virginia-Highland and Grant Park. The Atlanta BeltLine&rsquo;s Eastside Trail has transformed the neighborhoods around it, and living near it gives you a walking and biking connection between several popular areas. Buckhead, to the north, offers high-rise living and a concentration of offices, while neighborhoods south and east of downtown are often more affordable.</p><p>Traffic is a serious consideration. Many residents drive, and the highways can be congested at rush hour. <strong>If you can live near a MARTA rail station that serves your job</strong>, you may be able to skip much of the gridlock.</p>',
    'neighborhoods' => [
