      ['q' => 'How do I get from the Atlanta airport to the city?', 'a' => 'MARTA\'s Red and Gold lines run directly from Hartsfield-Jackson to Downtown and Midtown, often faster than driving at rush hour.'],
      ['q' => 'Is the BeltLine a good place to look for a sublet?', 'a' => 'Many renters love it for the trails, restaurants, and community feel. Neighborhoods along the Eastside Trail, like Old Fourth Ward and Inman Park, tend to be in high demand.'],
    ],
  ],

  'ma/boston' => [
    'name'   => 'Boston',
    'state'  => 'MA',
    'type'   => 'metro',
    'lat'    => 42.3601, 'lng' => -71.0589,
    'tagline'=> 'Furnished summer sublets and rotation housing across Boston, Cambridge, and Somerville, timed around the city\'s famous September 1 move-in.',
    'intro'  => '<p>Boston&rsquo;s rental market has a rhythm unlike any other US city. A large share of leases start on September 1, aligned with the academic year, which creates a massive annual turnover and a strong summer sublet season as students head home and want to cover their rent. If you need housing between May and August, Boston and Cambridge can be surprisingly good places to find it.</p><p>The city is home to world-renowned universities and hospitals, and short-term demand comes from medical residents, travel nurses, visiting researchers, summer interns, and students. The Longwood Medical Area, Kendall Square in Cambridge, and the Seaport are major hubs. Neighboring Cambridge and Somerville are effectively part of the same rental market, and many short-term renters search across all three cities at once.</p><p>Housing is a mix of classic triple-deckers, brownstones, and newer buildings. <strong>Plan around the September 1 turnover</strong>: if your stay crosses that date, confirm that your sublet runs through it, because finding something new in late August is notoriously difficult.</p>',
    'neighborhoods' => [
      ['name' => 'Back Bay', 'blurb' => 'Elegant brownstones, Newbury Street shopping, and Green and Orange Line access. Central and walkable, with prices to match.'],
      ['name' => 'South End', 'blurb' => 'Victorian rowhouses, restaurants, and parks, close to Back Bay and Boston Medical Center.'],
      ['name' => 'Jamaica Plain', 'blurb' => 'Green, diverse neighborhood near Jamaica Pond and the Arnold Arboretum, on the Orange Line. Popular with medical staff working in Longwood.'],
      ['name' => 'Allston-Brighton', 'blurb' => 'Student-heavy area near Boston University and Boston College, on the Green Line. Lots of summer sublets in shared apartments.'],
      ['name' => 'Fenway-Kenmore', 'blurb' => 'Around Fenway Park and next to the Longwood Medical Area, with many students and hospital workers.'],
      ['name' => 'Cambridge', 'blurb' => 'A separate city across the Charles River, home to Harvard and MIT, with Red Line stops in Harvard Square, Central Square, and Kendall Square.'],
      ['name' => 'Somerville', 'blurb' => 'Dense, lively city next to Cambridge, with triple-deckers, squares full of restaurants, and Red Line and Green Line access.'],
    ],
    'prices' => '<p>Boston is one of the most expensive rental markets in the country. Furnished private rooms in shared apartments commonly advertise somewhere in the $1,200&ndash;$2,000 per month range, while entire furnished studios and one-bedrooms in central neighborhoods typically run higher, often $2,800 or more.</p><p>Summer sublets can sometimes be found below these ranges as students try to cover their rent. These are rough figures, not official data &mdash; prices vary by season, neighborhood and furnishing, so compare current listings.</p>',
    'transit'=> '<p>The MBTA, known as the T, runs the Red, Orange, Blue, and Green subway lines, plus buses, the Silver Line bus rapid transit, commuter rail, and ferries. Many residents live without a car, and the core of the city is compact and walkable.</p><p>Boston Logan International Airport is close to downtown, reachable by the Blue Line (with a shuttle bus) or the Silver Line from South Station. Parking is scarce in many neighborhoods and often requires a resident permit.</p>',
    'tips' => [
      'interns'  => 'Summer interns benefit from Boston\'s student sublet season, when many furnished rooms open up from May to August. Kendall Square, the Seaport, and the Financial District are major hubs, so pick a place along the matching T line.',
      'nurses'   => 'Major employers include Massachusetts General Hospital, Brigham and Women\'s Hospital, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston Children\'s Hospital, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston Medical Center, and Tufts Medical Center. Jamaica Plain, Mission Hill, and Fenway are convenient for the Longwood Medical Area.',
      'students' => 'Harvard, MIT, Boston University, Northeastern, Boston College, Tufts, Emerson, and Suffolk are among the many institutions here. Summer sublets are abundant near these campuses.',
      'seasonal' => 'Most leases turn over on September 1, creating a crowded moving day and a busy summer sublet season. Late spring is a great time to find summer housing, while late August is the hardest time to search.',
    ],
    'faqs' => [
      ['q' => 'Why do so many Boston leases start on September 1?', 'a' => 'The large student population has tied much of the market to the academic year. As a result, many leases begin and end on September 1, which drives a big summer sublet market.'],
      ['q' => 'Is Cambridge or Somerville a good alternative to Boston?', 'a' => 'Yes. Both are separate cities with good T access, lively neighborhoods, and lots of short-term options, especially near Harvard, MIT, and Tufts.'],
      ['q' => 'Do I need a car for a short stay in Boston?', 'a' => 'Usually not. The T, buses, bike share, and walkable neighborhoods make car-free living easy, and parking can be hard to find.'],
    ],
  ],

  'az/phoenix' => [
