    'lat'    => 37.7749, 'lng' => -122.4194,
    'tagline'=> 'Furnished rooms and apartments for tech interns, medical rotations, and relocations, across San Francisco\'s hilly, fog-touched neighborhoods.',
    'intro'  => '<p>San Francisco is compact, hilly, and famously expensive, and furnished sublets are a common way to bridge the gap between arriving and finding a long-term lease. Tech interns, startup employees, medical trainees at UCSF, and professionals relocating to the Bay Area all compete for a limited supply of housing, so flexibility on neighborhood and move-in date helps.</p><p>The city&rsquo;s microclimates are real. The Mission and Potrero Hill tend to be sunnier, while the Sunset and Richmond districts near the ocean are often foggy and cool, even in summer. Pack layers no matter where you stay. Many people working in Silicon Valley also live in the city and commute south, while others prefer the East Bay, where BART makes Oakland and Berkeley practical alternatives.</p><p>Many apartments are in older Victorian and Edwardian buildings, often without in-unit laundry or dedicated parking. San Francisco has strong tenant protections and rules for short-term rentals under 30 days, so most sublets run a month or longer. <strong>Confirm that the primary tenant has permission to sublet</strong> and get your agreement in writing.</p>',
    'neighborhoods' => [
      ['name' => 'Mission District', 'blurb' => 'Sunny, vibrant neighborhood known for murals, taquerias, and Dolores Park, with BART stations on Mission Street.'],
      ['name' => 'Noe Valley', 'blurb' => 'Quiet, family-friendly neighborhood with a village-like main street and the J Church Muni line.'],
      ['name' => 'Inner Sunset', 'blurb' => 'Next to Golden Gate Park and UCSF\'s Parnassus campus, served by the N Judah Muni line. Popular with medical students and staff.'],
      ['name' => 'Hayes Valley', 'blurb' => 'Central neighborhood with boutiques, restaurants, and easy access to Market Street transit.'],
      ['name' => 'Nob Hill', 'blurb' => 'Historic hilltop neighborhood with cable cars, grand hotels, and older apartment buildings close to downtown.'],
      ['name' => 'Mission Bay', 'blurb' => 'Newer neighborhood of modern apartments near UCSF\'s Mission Bay campus, the ballpark, and Caltrain.'],
      ['name' => 'Inner Richmond', 'blurb' => 'Residential area north of Golden Gate Park known for its diverse restaurants along Clement Street, often foggy and cool.'],
    ],
    'prices' => '<p>San Francisco is among the most expensive rental markets in the country. Furnished private rooms in shared apartments commonly advertise somewhere in the $1,300&ndash;$2,300 per month range, while entire furnished studios and one-bedrooms typically run higher, often $3,000 or more in popular neighborhoods.</p><p>These are approximate ranges, not official data. Prices vary by season, neighborhood and furnishing, and summer intern season is especially competitive, so compare current listings before deciding.</p>',
    'transit'=> '<p>Muni runs buses, light rail, historic streetcars, and the iconic cable cars throughout the city. BART connects San Francisco to the East Bay and to San Francisco International Airport, while Caltrain runs down the Peninsula to Silicon Valley from its station in SoMa. Many residents live without a car, and parking can be hard to find.</p><p>San Francisco International Airport (SFO) is reachable by BART, and Oakland International Airport is another option across the bay.</p>',
    'tips' => [
      'interns'  => 'Many tech internships are in SoMa, the Financial District, and Mission Bay, while others are down the Peninsula in Silicon Valley. If you will commute south, look near Caltrain or BART to make the trip manageable.',
      'nurses'   => 'Major employers include UCSF Health, Zuckerberg San Francisco General Hospital, Kaiser Permanente San Francisco, and Sutter Health\'s California Pacific Medical Center. Stanford Health Care is down the Peninsula in Palo Alto.',
      'students' => 'UCSF, San Francisco State University, and the University of San Francisco are in the city, with UC Berkeley across the bay and Stanford down the Peninsula. Summer sublets near these campuses can be good value.',
      'seasonal' => 'Summer is the peak season for interns, and it is also often foggy and cool in much of the city. Demand stays relatively strong year-round because of the tech industry.',
    ],
    'faqs' => [
      ['q' => 'Is San Francisco cold in summer?', 'a' => 'Often, yes. Fog is common in summer, especially in the western neighborhoods. The Mission and other eastern areas tend to be sunnier and warmer.'],
      ['q' => 'Do I need a car in San Francisco?', 'a' => 'Usually not. Muni, BART, Caltrain, and walking cover most needs, and parking can be expensive and scarce.'],
      ['q' => 'What is the minimum stay for a sublet in San Francisco?', 'a' => 'San Francisco regulates short-term rentals under 30 days, so most sublets and furnished rentals are offered for at least a month.'],
    ],
  ],

